diff --git a/docs-site/docs/cookbook/cli-wrapper-spirit.md b/docs-site/docs/cookbook/cli-wrapper-spirit.md
index 37ed68d1..6a9fd394 100644
--- a/docs-site/docs/cookbook/cli-wrapper-spirit.md
+++ b/docs-site/docs/cookbook/cli-wrapper-spirit.md
@@ -68,6 +68,11 @@ agree. Other-home or unattributable containers remain report-only; label-less
 containers are ignored. Absent-CID T3 custody and empty dead-creator cgroups
 are reclaimed, never live/unknown custody or populated cgroups. Receipt rows
 name each result; `complete` does not mean every `left (<residue>)` row is gone.
+This metadata rule is a same-UID trust boundary, not unforgeable provenance:
+a same-user rootless container creator can forge it. Only empty process roots
+with dead creator PIDs are reclaimed; anonymous/live roots and shared parents
+(including `maos.slice`) stay. Residue is explicit in the receipt's `next_step`
+and CLI summary (`complete with residue: N left`), with exit 1 and unchanged schema.
 
 **Disk use:** Workspaces have no size quota. A runaway Worker can write until the filesystem holding the workspaces root is full; with the default root, that is the filesystem holding `MAOS_HOME`, including the Transparency Log, and retained workspaces accumulate across runs. Set `MAOS_WORKER_WORKSPACES_ROOT` to a directory on a separate filesystem to keep Worker output off the audit store. After each Worker run MAOS checks the free space on the workspace's filesystem and, below 1 GiB (1073741824 bytes), prints `maos run: WARNING: Worker workspaces filesystem at <workspace> has <n> bytes free (below 1073741824); …` to stderr; if the check itself fails it prints `maos run: WARNING: cannot check free space on Worker workspaces filesystem at <workspace>: <error>`. The warning neither refuses nor stops the run, and it is not journaled. The missing size cap is a claim boundary (`RELEASE-HOLDS.md`, Claim boundaries row 20): MAOS does not claim that a Worker's disk use is bounded.
 
diff --git a/docs-site/docs/run-maos.md b/docs-site/docs/run-maos.md
index 43141def..c3c0b27c 100644
--- a/docs-site/docs/run-maos.md
+++ b/docs-site/docs/run-maos.md
@@ -117,14 +117,24 @@ home's workspace roots must agree. Other-home or unattributable labelled
 containers are only reported with a removal command; label-less containers
 are ignored. Empty dead-creator `maos.slice/spirit-*` leaves are removed, never
 populated or live-creator leaves and never by killing. Private T3 custody is
-removed only for confirmed-absent CIDs, then empty process/state roots;
-unknown/live custody is retained. Dry-run custody lines are conditional on
-that absence check and invoke only read-only `ps`/`inspect`. Receipts add
+removed only for confirmed-absent CIDs, then empty process roots with a
+positively absent creator PID. Live/anonymous process roots, shared state
+parents and `maos.slice` are retained; unknown/live spawn custody is retained.
+Dry-run spawn-custody lines are conditional on CID absence and use only read-only `ps`/`inspect`. Receipts add
 `podman://<CID>`, `cgroup://<path>` and custody-directory rows, without changing
 the schema: `left (<residue>)` can coexist with status `complete`, as with
 Worker workspace residue. Read every row. User-namespace mappings and podman
 storage/configuration are never edited.
 
+Matching metadata is not unforgeable proof: another container creator under the
+same rootless UID can forge the labels, name and mount. Same-UID runtime control
+is not a privilege boundary. Bare `podman` is resolved on trusted operator PATH.
+Surviving MAOS-written receipt rows produce `complete with residue: N left` in
+the existing `next_step` string and final CLI line, with exit 1; receipt status
+vocabulary and schema remain unchanged. HOME must be set and absolute; HOME or
+ancestor purge roots and `..` paths are refused. Legacy ancestors are checked
+without following symlinks; purge never uses the shared HOME-unset `/tmp` fallback.
+
 `maos purge --keep-log --yes` checkpoints and keeps the Transparency Log. This
 also keeps shared-memory rows and the principal namespace index when they share
 that database, including capability-token rows and legacy `shell.turn` text.
